ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (4.0.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * **Protocol 3.** The uplink budget, the `/fleetless/uplink_kbps` override
    topic and the `bridge_pressure` datapoint are gone; a cloud speaking
    protocol 2 will refuse this package, and this package will refuse it.
  * **No upload size cap of the bridge's own.** A robot's assets are charged
    against a one-gigabyte store the cloud alone can evaluate — it knows what
    the robot's other files already occupy and this process does not. So every
    mesh and texture is attempted, and an upload the store has no room for is
    reported as `refused`, carrying the cloud's own three numbers. Nothing is
    read into memory to find this out: the upload streams, as it already did.
  * **A backfilled datapoint says so.** A sample replayed after a
    disconnect carries `backfill`, so an hour of buffered history arriving at
    once no longer reads to the cloud as an hour of lag on a link that is fine.
  * **A rotated token stops the bridge instead of reconnecting forever.** The
    cloud closes the socket with its own code when a token is rotated in the
    console; the log says to start the bridge with the new one, and the exit
    code is 2, so the launch file does not respawn into the same wall.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (3.2.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * **The protocol version comes from the vendored contracts now**, together
    with the version window the cloud serves and the newest bridge release
    the contracts knew about. It used to be a literal typed on both sides of
    the wire, with one test standing between them and drift.
  * **A refused protocol version no longer exits straight away.** The cloud
    can refuse the version this package speaks; the fix is a newer package,
    which the running process could never load. It waits one to two minutes
    and then exits, so the launch file's respawn retries every couple of
    minutes instead of every five seconds — and a fresh process picks up an
    upgraded package by itself. The exit code is still 2.
  * **A deprecated protocol version is warned about once per process**, with
    the date the cloud stops serving it and the newest release to upgrade to.
    Once, not once per reconnect: the date does not move in between.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (3.1.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * **Relicensed to the Apache License 2.0.** The package's installed
    copyright file — `/usr/share/doc/ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge/copyright`,
    the file Debian policy designates for this question — now declares
    Apache-2.0 and points at the public source. The source tree carries
    `LICENSE`, `NOTICE` and an SPDX header on every source file to match.
    Only `ros-humble-fleetless-bridge` was ever published before this
    version — up to and including 3.0.0, its copyright declared the software
    closed and not redistributable, and named a source host outside this
    repository. `ros-jazzy-fleetless-bridge` and `ros-lyrical-fleetless-bridge`
    were never published before this version, so it is their first release
    and their whole published history is Apache-2.0. `LICENSE` and `NOTICE`
    are now installed with the package: the Apache licence asks a
    redistributor to pass the NOTICE file on, which they cannot do from a
    .deb that does not contain it.
  * **The cloud transport and the live-video stack were both rewritten, so
    this is a minor version, not a patch.** `websockets` is replaced by
    `python3-aiohttp` for the cloud connection, and the LiveKit SDK is
    replaced entirely by `python3-aiortc` plus this package's own JSON
    signalling client — the SDK's wheel is pip-only, with a protobuf floor
    no supported Ubuntu ships, which is what kept this package off the ROS
    index. `bitrate_kbps`, pause-on-no-viewer and TURN relay all still work,
    proved directly against aiortc rather than through the SDK. No pip
    anywhere any more: `debian/postinst` installed three packages from PyPI
    as root at configure time as recently as this same package's previous
    build on Humble (`3.0.0`, still the only version ever published); this
    one Depends on apt packages exclusively, and `rosdep install` needs no
    PEP 668 override on any of the three distributions this package builds
    for. 3.0.1 was never published — this version replaces it as the tree's
    next release.
  * **Removing a driven publisher no longer loses its failsafe.** Removing,
    retargeting or stopping a publisher that had been published to sent its
    failsafe and destroyed the publisher in the same breath. A sample the
    middleware dropped on first send then had no writer left to resend it,
    and the robot kept its last command. The publisher now stays until every
    reliable subscriber has acknowledged that failsafe, for at most four
    seconds, and waiting for it holds up no other publisher's watchdog --
    except when a driven publisher changes its message type on the same
    topic, where the old one gets at most half a second before it makes
    way. Shutdown takes up to those four seconds longer when a subscriber
    does not answer.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (3.0.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * **The configuration format changed, and this version does not read the
    old one.** A robot's configuration is now a `fleetless.yaml` document:
    six sections keyed by slug rather than five arrays of objects carrying a
    `slug` field, names spelled with underscores rather than dashes, and a
    `fleetless: 1` header the bridge refuses a frame without. A 2.x bridge
    and a 3.x cloud cannot talk to each other in either direction,
    which is why this is a major version. Upgrade the cloud and the robot in
    one window.
  * Actions, services and publishers send a **message template**. The
    developer writes the Goal, Request or published message literally and
    marks with `${name}` the positions a caller may fill; the bridge
    substitutes at those positions and nowhere else. A field written `0.0` in
    the configuration is `0.0` on the wire and no client can reach it — the
    format's safety story is structural now rather than a rule that has to
    hold. `params.unflatten` and the dotted field paths it existed for are
    gone with it.
  * A **failsafe** is a group: `{timeout_ms, message}`, the deadline sitting
    beside the message it exists to trigger. Its message must resolve with no
    placeholder left in it, and the bridge checks that **after** following a
    reference into the shared `messages:` map — contracts states plainly that
    it cannot answer that half, because a schema over one publisher cannot
    see the map. This is the only place the reference case is caught before a
    robot arms a failsafe it could never send.
  * `rate_throttle_hz` replaces the `rate: {mode, hz}` object: a plain
    ceiling, 0 to 20, where **zero or absent means no throttling at all**.
    That is a behaviour change and it is the one worth reading twice. The old
    `on_change` mode dropped a sample equal to the last one sent; "no
    throttling" does not. A battery holding a steady 87 % now reports every
    sample, where a bridge that treated the two as the same would have gone
    silent for hours and looked like a robot that had stopped.
  * `buffer` becomes `retention`: `enabled` and `max_buffer_values` are the
    disconnect buffer this bridge already had. `retention.interval_seconds`
    is parsed and deliberately never read — it decides how often the *cloud*
    writes to history, which is not a decision this side can make.
  * Camera credentials arrive **inline** in `doc.cameras[].source.credentials`.
    The `credentials` sibling on the config frame is gone with the store
    behind it, and an explicit block still beats userinfo in the URL. The
    `/dev/` containment rule and the URL scheme restrictions are unchanged
    and re-derived here on purpose: the libraries these values reach also
    serve `file:` and `ftp:`.
  * `snapshot_interval_ms` becomes `snapshot_interval_seconds`, 1 to 3600.
    A unit change, not a rename — the same number means something a thousand
    times different, so every site was re-read rather than renamed.
  * `PRESSURE_SLUG` was `bridge-pressure` here and `bridge_pressure` in the
    contracts. The re-vendored schema caught the drift. A robot on this
    version sends the underscore spelling, which a 2.x cloud does not
    know — and the symptom is a datapoint that silently never arrives, not an
    error.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (2.2.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * The pressure counters 2.1.0 started collecting are now on the wire: a
    `bridge-pressure` datapoint -- per-tier sent/bytes/drops/high-water,
    the measured send rate, the snapshot byte budget, and video/uplink
    state -- goes out every 10 seconds while connected, on the same
    reserved-slug path as `bridge-state`, so history, realtime, REST and
    MCP exposure fall out of the ordinary datapoint machinery for free.
    No frame while disconnected: an honest gap, same as every datapoint.
  * Tier 2's `high_water` is now truly per-session, not a stale peak
    carried over from whichever connection last touched it. The two
    queues that feed it -- live samples and camera state -- outlive the
    session they were built for, so a dropped connection now baselines
    both to their own current depth as *that* session ends, before the
    next one's writer is even built. Not to zero: a camera-state backlog
    that piled up while nothing was connected at all is real depth, and
    still shows up correctly -- at its actual size -- on the very first
    frame of the reconnect that finally gets to drain it.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (2.1.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * One prioritized writer now owns the socket. Every outgoing frame --
    hello, pongs, config acknowledgements, job outcomes, live telemetry,
    camera state, developer tooling, buffered history and snapshots -- is
    ranked into one of six tiers (0 highest, session liveness, down to 5,
    snapshots) and sent in that strict order, so a slow link starves the
    cheapest thing first instead of whatever happened to be queued first.
    A pong or a job outcome can no longer get stuck behind a backlog drain
    or a large snapshot the way 2.0.2 could only patch around for
    snapshots specifically.
  * Snapshots are now fitted to the link, not just to the wire format.
    The writer measures the actual send rate from the frames large enough
    to have really left the robot -- a small frame is accepted into the
    socket's write buffer and says nothing about the link -- and asks for
    a snapshot sized to fit inside the same occupancy budget 2.0.2
    introduced. So a slow robot gets a smaller snapshot instead of the
    same flat 1.5 MiB every time, and a fast one is no longer held back by
    a bound that only ever had to protect the slow case. As in 2.0.2, the
    first snapshot of a session is what establishes the rate: it goes out
    at the wire-format bound, and every later one is fitted to what that
    one measured.
  * Buffered history (a reconnect's backlog) is its own tier, drained
    oldest first and only once nothing higher-priority is waiting. The
    50 ms floor between backfill sends is retired -- it existed only to
    keep backfill from crowding out live traffic, and that is now
    structural: live traffic always sits in a higher tier and preempts
    the drain between any two backfill items.
  * A developer can now configure an uplink budget for live video, via
    the `FLEETLESS_UPLINK_KBPS` environment variable or the
    `/fleetless/uplink_kbps` topic (the topic always wins once anything
    has been published on it, and the control socket keeps a reserve no
    matter how small the budget is). A `camera_start` that would push the
    running total over budget is refused with an honest `camera_state`
    rather than opened and left to fight over bandwidth with what is
    already live; lowering the budget below what is already running stops
    streams newest-first until it fits again.
  * A raw camera frame is converted to BGR only when something will
    actually use it -- a live viewer or a snapshot request due right now.
    A camera nobody is watching and nobody is snapshotting no longer pays
    that conversion cost on every frame, which is most of them on a robot
    with several cameras configured but only one in view at a time.
  * The writer's own per-tier counters (frames sent, bytes sent, drops,
    queue high-water mark), its measured send rate and the uplink budget's
    state are now collected continuously. Nothing consumes them on the
    wire yet -- that is the console pressure indicator, still to come --
    but the bridge no longer has to be rebuilt to start measuring its own
    backpressure.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (2.0.2-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * A snapshot no longer holds the socket long enough to end the session.
    `websockets` serializes sends, so a large binary frame is time the
    `pong` behind it is not being sent, and the cloud closes after three
    unanswered pings -- about six seconds for everything. The snapshot pump
    now measures what the socket actually manages and drops a frame that
    would occupy it for more than two seconds, sending one anyway after
    five consecutive drops so the estimate can never latch off.
  * `camera.SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES` bounds the same frame at 1.5 MiB, against
    the ws library's 2 MiB ceiling. That bound is about the frame format
    and knows nothing about the link: on a 151 kB/s uplink it
    is ten seconds of exclusive use of the socket that also carries the
    control channel.
  * Datapoints, jobs, commands and live video are unaffected -- live video
    goes through LiveKit and never touches this socket.
  * Found while verifying 2.0.1 on a real robot: taking the config apply off the
    receive loop stretched the sessions from 12-20 s to nearly three
    minutes without making them survive, which is what made the second,
    unrelated cause visible.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 15:30:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (2.0.1-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * A slow config apply no longer costs the connection. `config`,
    `introspect_request` and `type_request` were the only cloud frames the
    bridge awaited inline in its receive loop; everything else is
    dispatched and answered later. An apply that outlasted the cloud's pong
    deadline therefore took the whole session down: the `ping` queued
    behind it was never read, so no `pong` was sent, the cloud closed the
    socket, and the reconnect was answered with the same config -- a loop
    the robot could not leave on its own.
  * Found on a real robot with one camera configured on a topic carrying 9.44 MB
    frames at 2 Hz (19.3 MB/s). Thirteen minutes connected before the
    camera was published, then 7 connects and 18 pong timeouts in 100
    seconds after it. Nothing in the suite carries a message that size, and
    no rehearsal subscribes to a real camera, so no test could have found
    it -- the fix ships with two that would have.
  * The three frames now run on one FIFO worker, so a config still cannot
    overtake its predecessor, and a send failure ends the session exactly
    as it did before.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (2.0.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * Wire protocol bumped 1 -> 2 (the contracts' `PROTOCOL_VERSION`): `config_applied.errors` entries gained `kind` and
    `code` beside `message`. The cloud's handshake check is `!==`, not a
    floor, so a bridge announcing any other version is refused outright --
    no telemetry, no cameras, no jobs -- until this package is installed.
    Major, not patch: this is the wire itself changing shape, not a fix
    inside one version of it, and a robot on the previous version is not
    compatible with a cloud that requires this one.
  * This is the package operators upgrade to when `getting-started.md`'s
    "Upgrading an already-connected fleet" warning tells them to run
    `sudo apt-get install --only-upgrade ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge` --
    without this entry that command had nothing newer to offer, and
    `bridge_version` read `1.0.1` for an upgraded and an un-upgraded robot
    alike, so the one instrument an operator has during the outage window
    could not tell the two states apart.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (1.0.1-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * Meshes resolve again on a workspace built with `colcon build
    --symlink-install`. The containment check that closes the package://
    traversal compared the *physical* path (os.path.realpath) against the
    share directory -- and colcon installs every file as a symlink back
    into the source tree, so each mesh resolved to <ws>/src/... and failed
    containment against <ws>/install/.../share/...
  * The failure mode was the expensive part, not the bug: os.path.isfile
    was true, the bytes were readable, and the reference was reported as
    unresolvable -- which the console renders as "not found in the robot's
    workspace. Add these to the robot's workspace, then sync again." Ten
    meshes that were in the workspace, named individually, with a remedy
    that cannot work. Found while testing the console against a real robot.
  * The check is lexical now (normpath, no symlink following), so every
    traversal test still fails closed; the residual (a
    symlink inside an installed package pointing outside it) is documented
    at the check rather than implied away.
  * The launch file now sets PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1. cv_bridge is a C
    extension built against numpy 1.x, so this package installs numpy
    1.26.4 to /usr/local -- but a `pip install --user` in the operating
    account's own home puts ~/.local ahead of /usr/local on sys.path, and
    on one robot that meant numpy 2.2.6. The result was the worst available
    shape: CvBridge still imports, the bridge starts, connects and reports
    healthy, and the first real imgmsg_to_cv2 call dies with
    'AttributeError: _ARRAY_API not found' -- every camera on the robot
    broken, and nothing says so until someone opens one. Seen on a real
    robot.

 -- Andre Dehne <andre@dehne-robotik.de>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 22:55:00 +0200

ros-@ROS_DISTRO@-fleetless-bridge (1.0.0-0@DEB_CODENAME@) @DEB_CODENAME@; urgency=medium

  * First published build under the project's own apt repository. Replaces
    the 0.2.0 package that predates the rebuild and shares no module
    structure with this package. That one existed for Humble only, as
    0.2.0-0jammy; on every other distribution this entry is the first
    build there has ever been, and there was nothing to replace.

